EarthMapCreatorO
============================
Generate real-Earth maps for Vintage Story.

Original code by tlm9201. Heavily modified and improved, and the bash script image processing pipeline was rewritten in python for windows+linux compat and ease of use.
Not a fork because its functionally impossible to pull from upstream (due to the extent of rewrite) and the merge conflicts got annoying.

https://github.com/tlm9201/vs-earth-map-creator

Requirements
-------------------
- Visual Studio 2022 with .NET 10 SDK (or `dotnet` CLI)
- Vintage Story 1.22.7 or a compatible .NET 10 game installation
- Python 3 and packages in Geo/requirements.txt for map generation
- ImageMagick (optional; river post-processing falls back to Pillow)

Visual Studio build
-------------------
Open EarthMapCreatorO.sln and build the EarthMapCreator project. Set VINTAGE_STORY
to the game installation directory, or copy
EarthMapCreator/Properties/localSettings.props.example to localSettings.props and
set GameDirectory and DataFolder there. That local file is not committed.
Debug builds appear under EarthMapCreator/bin/Debug/Mods/earthmapcreatoro/.
Release builds create `Releases/earthmapcreatoro_<version>.zip`. Mod metadata is
generated into the build output from the properties at the top of
`EarthMapCreator.csproj`; update `<ModVersion>` there to bump the version.
The Client and Server profiles use the normal Vintage Story data folder, preserving
credentials, saves, and mod configuration. Run the Geo pipeline first; Debug
builds read `earthmap.regions` directly from `Geo/work/build/` without copying
the multi-gigabyte artifact. Release builds use the normal Vintage Story
`EarthMap` folder.

Release builds embed path-mapped symbols for useful crash-log line numbers.
Paths to this repository are replaced with /_/EarthMapCreator; SourceLink is
disabled, and standalone PDBs are excluded from the ZIP. Check compiled release
artifacts for machine paths before sharing them.

Directories
-------------------
.
├── EarthMapCreator   - Visual Studio mod project
└── Geo               - independent geospatial processing scripts

Python Pipeline (Geo/)
-------------------
The pipeline downloads some datasets automatically, but several large files must be
placed manually in Geo/datasets/ before running:

  ds75_grd.zip              GMTED 250m DEM (topography)
                            https://edcintl.cr.usgs.gov/downloads/sciweb1/shared/topo/downloads/GMTED/Grid_ZipFiles/ds75_grd.zip

  gebco_2025_sub_ice_topo.zip  GEBCO bathymetry (or extract the .nc and place it directly)
                            https://www.gebco.net/data_and_products/gridded_bathymetry_data/


  ne_10m_lakes.zip          Natural Earth lakes + reservoirs (the pipeline filters reservoirs)
                            https://www.naturalearthdata.com/downloads/10m-physical-vectors/

  gm_ve_v1.zip              Global vegetation canopy cover
                            https://www.earthenv.org/vegetation

  HydroRIVERS_v10_na_shp/  Extract the North/Central America HydroRIVERS
                            shapefile here, including .shp, .shx, .dbf, .prj:
                            https://www.hydrosheds.org/products/hydrorivers

The following are downloaded automatically on first run and cached:
  - OSM land polygons
  - CHELSA V2.1 1981–2010 monthly climatologies at 30 arc-seconds (~1 km):
    12 TIFFs each for `tas`, `pr`, and `pet` (36 files, roughly 11 GB total).
    Cached as `CHELSA_{variable}_{MM}_1981-2010_V.2.1.tif` in `Geo/datasets/`.
    Source: https://chelsa-climate.org/datasets/chelsa_climatologies
    Files: https://os.unil.cloud.switch.ch/chelsa02/chelsa/global/climatologies/


Once datasets are in place:

  0. The python scripts work on Windows or Linux, but you must have python and pip installed.

  1. Install Python dependencies:
       cd Geo/
       pip install -r requirements.txt

  2. Edit Geo/config.py to set your bounding box (LAT_MIN/MAX, LON_MIN/MAX),
     output resolution (FINAL_WIDTH, FINAL_LENGTH — must be multiples of 512),
     and projection. You can use sites such as https://boundingbox.klokantech.com/
	 to find a bounding box for the desired map.

  3. Run the pipeline:
       python launch.py

  4. Nine PNGs for inspection, `earthmap.regions` (EMREGION v3), and
     `earthclimate.bin` for the mod are written to Geo/work/build/.

The climate stage samples CHELSA V2.1 1981–2010 monthly temperature (`tas`,
scaled kelvin), precipitation (`pr`) and Penman–Monteith potential
evapotranspiration (`pet`) at 32-block intervals on the final output grid.
Precipitation and PET are in kg/m²/month (numerically mm/month); the twelve
monthly totals are summed for each annual value. TIFF scale, offset, and
NoData metadata are honored. EMCL v2 contains a 24-byte little-endian header
(`EMCL`, version 2, width, height, spacing, float32 lapse), followed by
Z-major samples of twelve signed temperatures (tenths of °C), annual
precipitation and annual PET (unsigned millimetres), and the mean terrain Y of
the sample's 32×32 cell (unsigned tenths of a source block; ocean counts as
sea level). The climate stage reads the final `heightmap.png`, `landmask.png`
and `lake_mask.png`, and fits the lapse (°C per source block) from local
CHELSA temperature/terrain differences (about -0.12 on the current map).
Sea samples use the nearest available coastal CHELSA value. Dimensions must
match `earthmap.regions`; the mod rejects missing, mismatched, or EMCL v1
climate files at startup.

Runtime temperature at a block adds `lapse × (block Y − cell terrain Y)`, with
block Y converted to source Y for taller worlds. CHELSA describes air at the
smoothed terrain, so summits are colder than their cell and valleys warmer.
This also matters for snow: Vintage Story's snow accumulation samples each
512-block region at the world's sea level and at world top, then interpolates
by height. The mod's climate callback supplies a distinct temperature for
each height; it does not inherit vanilla's altitude adjustment. Snowmelt also
depends on hourly temperature variation, rainfall, snow-layer ticks, and saved
snow state. A colder summit does not guarantee snow throughout spring; check
the actual world's sea level and seasonal weather before predicting duration.

On server startup the mod reapplies the region file's sea-level datum during
asset finalization, after vanilla terrain setup (which otherwise resets it to
110 in a 256-high world). This keeps the weather snow sampler's lower Y,
snow-accumulation interpolation, rainfall scaling, and map terrain on the same
datum. Vanilla `GenSnowLayer` otherwise cools the projected CHELSA climate map
a second time by altitude and places snow in locations warmer than its runtime
melting threshold; the snow placement hook uses CHELSA's surface temperature
without that second adjustment. Existing snow placed in already generated
chunks is not rebuilt by this change; newly generated chunks use it.

Worldgen block layers sample annual temperature and vegetation wetness
`min(P/(P+max(PET,1)), P/200, 1)`. A projected VS climate map lets built-in
trees, shrubs, and patches continue using their current JSON selection
thresholds. Both server and client use the monthly temperature curve: the
universal mod transfers the compressed climate grid in 32 KiB chunks after
joining, so clients need the mod but no local map files. Server generation
keeps its own climate grid when an integrated client receives the transfer.
Until transfer finishes, client temperature uses vanilla behavior. Weather
precipitation noise and weather-event selection use annual P via `P/(P+800)`,
a provisional normalization rather than measured rain frequency. Weather
patterns may continue to use vegetation wetness.

Vanilla worldgen applies a sea-level temperature lapse to packed climate
bytes. CHELSA's packed values already describe local terrain, so the mod
removes that second cooling for tree and shrub selection, structure placement,
climate-limited deposits, creatures, ponds, schematic block layers, snow and
surface patches. Server and client climate accessors instead expose the EMCL
annual temperature plus its cell-relative lapse at the requested Y; the
monthly callback still supplies seasonal, diurnal and weather variation.
Client block and world colormaps use the same terrain-referenced temperature
while retaining vanilla precipitation and season coloring. These corrections
change generation and new client tints; already generated terrain is not
rewritten.

The server-side `topsoil-patch.json` adjusts Vintage Story's blocklayers for
CHELSA's annual P/PET wetness scale. At wetness ≥ 0.15 and fertility ≥ 0.10,
warm low-fertility ground gets sparse low-fertility grass soil over low-fertility
subsoil rather than an infertile sand stack; below 0.15 the dry sand/gravel
layers remain. The lowest tallgrass moisture threshold is also 0.15.

Surface selection uses the warmest CHELSA month rather than world height.
Snowpack sites—warmest month below `SnowpackWarmestMonthTemperature` in the
server's `ModConfig/EarthMapCreator.json` (default 5 °C)—get a -20 °C layer
temperature, producing full snow blocks over gravel. This marks regions whose
summers stay near freezing. Colorado's permanent snow survives only in
microclimates below the 32-block climate grid: the coldest sample on the
current map (Mt Elbert) has a warmest month of about 8 °C, and Arapaho Glacier
samples about 11.3 °C. The current map therefore has no snowpack at the
default. Warmer sites use a layer temperature of at
least -10 °C. Below 12 °C, block-layer moisture is set
to zero, producing tundra gravel without grass. Seasonal cold deserts with an
annual mean below 10 °C and wetness below 0.19 also use dry gravel (including
Alamosa); wetter cold sites can keep sparse grass. Vanilla cools each deeper
layer by its height; CHELSA already includes altitude, so a Harmony
transpiler holds the whole stack at the column's layer temperature. All
glacier-ice layers and the high-Y and cold-mountain gravel layers are removed.
Stored P/PET and weather stay unchanged. Regenerate existing chunks to see the
updated surface and subsurface.

Vanilla surface block patches also read the packed climate map. Their
temperature check must not apply a second altitude correction to CHELSA's
terrain-level temperatures: doing so made mild elevated steppe too cold for
vanilla's loose-stone patches (minimum -2 °C). The patched check preserves
each patch's configured temperature and moisture limits. Flint surface pieces
remain tied to vanilla flint deposits in sandstone, shale, claystone, chalk,
or limestone; the change does not generate flint over other rock types.
Already generated chunks keep their existing surface blocks.

The region-store v3 header records the sea-level Y from `Geo/config.py`; v2
files must be rebuilt so the mod can read the same datum used for terrain,
bathymetry, rivers, and oceans. Stop any game/server using the existing region
file before running the pipeline so Windows can replace it.
A new map takes effect for newly generated chunks; regenerate
existing chunks to update their climate-derived terrain and vegetation.

Natural Earth supplies the lake polygons. Records classified as `Lake` or
`Alkaline Lake` are candidates; `Reservoir` records are excluded. Natural
Earth 5.0.0 nevertheless labels several reservoirs as `Lake`, including Lake
Havasu. The pipeline therefore also excludes the current-map features that
OpenStreetMap classifies as `water=reservoir`, using a reviewed correction list
keyed by stable Natural Earth Wikidata ID where available and exact name
otherwise; regeneration does not query OSM. This is not a complete global
reservoir classifier: Natural Earth and OSM classifications can be incomplete
or change between dataset releases.
The selection is enforced while creating `crop_lakes.gpkg`, again when lakes
select GMTED rather than GEBCO topography, and again during final-grid lake
rasterization so stale staged files cannot reintroduce a reservoir. Natural
`Alkaline Lake` records remain included.

Lake shorelines are computed on the final output grid. The pipeline rasterizes
each connected lake to one waterline, derives a 1-12 block tapered depth, and
grades the surrounding bank over six blocks. `lake_mask.png` and the
region-store lake plane encode 0 = no lake, 255 = freshwater (`Lake`), and
128 = saltwater (`Alkaline Lake`, including the Salton Sea and Great Salt
Lake). For salt lakes classified only as `Lake`, add their exact Natural Earth
`name` values to `SALINE_LAKE_NAMES` in `Geo/config.py`. The empty default
keeps unlisted lakes freshwater; the list changes water type but does not add
missing polygons. Matching names apply to every polygon with that name. A
connected lake containing both types uses saltwater throughout. The game uses
saltwater blocks for value 128; all other lake values remain freshwater.
`lake_depth.png` stores depth in blocks. `heightmap.png`, `landmask.png`, and
both lake files share one final-grid footprint. `complete_topo.png` is
obsolete. Rerun `python launch.py` from `Geo/` after changing lake selection;
it recreates `work/crop_lakes.gpkg`, all final PNGs, `earthmap.regions`, and
`earthclimate.bin`. Previously generated maps retain their old polygons and
lake types.

Final-grid coastal correction follows lake rasterization and precedes river
fitting. Ocean pixels with missing or invalidly low encoded bathymetry (below
`BATHY_SCALE_MAXDEPTH`) are replaced with one-block-deep ocean: a bed at
sea level minus one and water at sea level. Dry land below sea level within
16 blocks of existing ocean is converted to the same shallow ocean by
clearing its land mask. Valid neighboring depths are not smoothed. Lake
surfaces and inland below-sea-level basins are left alone. Rebuild PNGs and
`earthmap.regions` to apply this correction; existing game chunks must also
be regenerated.

Rivers use the local HydroRIVERS North/Central America centerline dataset.
`RIVER_MIN_UPSTREAM_AREA_SQKM` in `Geo/config.py` selects reaches by upstream
drainage area (`UPLAND_SKM`); the default is 1,000 km² at 25,600 blocks per
axis. With `RIVER_AUTO_SCALE_UPSTREAM_AREA = True` (the default), the effective
threshold is multiplied by `max(1, 25600 / min(output width, output height))`.
A 12,800 × 12,800 map of the same region therefore uses 2,000 km²; a
10,240 × 10,240 map uses 2,500 km². Non-square maps use their shorter axis;
larger maps do not lower the configured threshold. Set the option to `False`
to hold the threshold fixed regardless of output dimensions.
The default full-size threshold selects roughly 30,000 reaches before the
mean-flow cutoff. `RIVER_MIN_FLOW_CMS` then excludes reaches whose long-term
mean discharge (`DIS_AV_CMS`) is below 1 m³/s, including the mapped zero-flow
channel through Badwater Basin. This removes many dry channels, but annual
mean flow does not identify seasonal rivers; some intermittent reaches can
remain and some small perennial reaches can be excluded. Tune both thresholds
in `Geo/config.py`. Selected reaches are clipped to the map extent and
simplified at half a final pixel before rasterization and
waterline fitting. Channel width uses a square-root curve: at
`RIVER_WIDTH_REFERENCE_FLOW_CMS` (5.4 m³/s), it gains one output block over the
`RIVER_MIN_WIDTH_PIXELS` minimum (3), capped by `RIVER_MAX_WIDTH_PIXELS` (8).
HydroRIVERS reports roughly 21.6 m³/s near Dodge City, Kansas; this gives
the Arkansas River a nominal five-block width there. Rasterized widths vary
by cross-section orientation and at joins. Cells are selected by their centers
so touching edges do not add a ninth column at the cap. These settings control
visual width, not a measured hydraulic width. HydroRIVERS has no mapped
riverbank polygons. Terminal reaches with at least 10,000 km² upstream area
can cross up to 30 km of continuous sea-level land to the nearest ocean cell.
This approximates pre-diversion flow in dry deltas (including the Colorado),
not a historically surveyed channel: a ridge or lake blocks the connection.
Tune `RIVER_COASTAL_CONNECTION_*` in `Geo/config.py` to change those limits.
Other centerlines drain toward lakes, coasts, or map edges.
An isotonic fit joins tributaries to shared levels. The HydroRIVERS
source-to-`NEXT_DOWN` order is retained through final raster fitting, so
downstream water never climbs a mapped reach even where the DEM rises.
River water is limited to adjacent dry-bank height; narrow high-water
crests and isolated low-water pockets at bends are carved across the
channel without raising banks or water. Every water column assigned to a
centerline sample shares its lowest cross-section level, while distinct
downstream samples retain discrete drops. Banks touching multiple water
levels grade from the highest adjacent column; natural low banks are never
raised.
Quantization permits flat stretches where the real gradient is below
one block. `river.png` is binary; `river_surface.png` stores absolute
water Y and `river_depth.png` stores 1–3 block depths. Rebuild all maps after
changing projection, bounds, scale, or river selection/width/depth settings in
`Geo/config.py`.

During block-layer generation, dry land within four blocks of a mapped river
channel upgrades only existing `soil-low-*` surface blocks to `soil-medium-*`,
keeping their grass coverage variant. The halo crosses chunk and map-region
boundaries, but does not affect riverbeds, lakes, oceans, existing medium soil,
or gravel/sand. It requires vegetation wetness ≥ 0.19 and a warmest month of
at least 12 °C; dry cold deserts and tundra therefore remain unaltered. This
does not change stored climate, river maps, or subsurface blocks. Regenerate
existing chunks to apply it.

The pipeline reads an extracted dataset from `Geo/datasets/`, or from
`~/Downloads/HydroRIVERS_v10_na_shp/` if it is already extracted there.
Only the final `=== Pipeline complete ===` message means all maps rebuilt.
The Salton Sea is an `Alkaline Lake` in Natural Earth and remains saline in
the final lake mask. Updating PNGs or `earthmap.regions` does
not change already generated game chunks. Restart the server with the new
region file and generate new terrain (or regenerate affected chunks) to
see a changed lake or river in an existing world.


The current `Geo/config.py` preset covers 30.59–49.00 N, 120.00–94.04 W
in Web Mercator, with a 25,600 × 25,600 block output (Vintage Story's
"25k blocks" preset). Terrain pixels store absolute Vintage Story Y:
zero metres and the ocean surface use `TERRAIN_SEA_LEVEL_Y` (currently 60),
and the highest valid elevation on the final output grid maps to Y=255. All
other elevations use the same linear metres-to-block scale, including terrain
below sea level. Bathymetry uses the same sea datum; its separate
`BATHY_SCALE_MAXDEPTH` (currently 50) is the deepest ocean-bed Y, while
`BATHY_EXAGGERATE_MIDPOINT` (currently 55) must lie between it and sea level.
The v3 region header carries the source sea level to the mod. On 256-high
worlds source Y is unchanged; on 384- and 512-high worlds, the mod linearly
maps absolute Y by `round(sourceY × (worldHeight - 1) / 255)`. The ocean
surface, terrain, ocean bed, lake surfaces and beds, and river surfaces and
beds use this same mapping. Vintage Story's sea level follows the mapped
datum (source Y=60 becomes Y=90 at height 384). Horizontal maps and climate
data are unchanged; reuse the same `earthmap.regions` and `earthclimate.bin`
without rerunning Python when choosing a different world height. A taller
world stretches existing 8-bit elevation steps, not adds new terrain detail;
changing an existing world's height requires regenerating its chunks.

Visual Studio debugging
-------------------
After running `python launch.py`, select `Client` or `Server` and press F5.
The Debug build fails if `earthmap.regions` is absent. The game retains
its normal AppData profile, credentials, saves, and `ModConfig`; the map
artifact is loaded directly from `Geo/work/build/`. Map dimensions are read
from the artifact header; no mod configuration dimensions are required.

Advanced Geology server test
-------------------
Set `VINTAGE_STORY` to the game installation directory, build the map with
`python Geo/launch.py`, then run:

    python ServerTests/run_ag_ores.py --ag-mod path/to/advancedgeology.zip

This opt-in test builds both the mod and a test-only server probe, launches an
isolated fresh world with AG, and searches a 32×32-chunk area near map center
for non-granite rock and a non-gem metal ore block after world generation.
It reports the found ore block code; failure or timeout returns a nonzero exit
status. The world and temporary probe mod are removed afterward. It does not
modify your normal Vintage Story saves or prove that every AG deposit type
generates.

Usage (in Vintage Story)
-------------------
1. Copy both `Geo/work/build/earthmap.regions` and `Geo/work/build/earthclimate.bin` into your VS data directory:
     Linux:   ~/.config/VintagestoryData/EarthMap/
     Windows: %APPDATA%\VintagestoryData\EarthMap\

2. Install the mod and create a world with the same dimensions as the generated
   map. The mod reads map dimensions from `earthmap.regions` and loads regions
   as needed.

   The map is placed so that chunk 0,0 (top-left of the world) aligns with
   the top-left of your PNG images. Map size must be a multiple of 512 blocks.
   If you create a world that is larger than the generated images, you may spawn in the void since the entire area outside the valid image data zone is simply not generated.
   Vintage story spawns you in the center of the total map dimensions and maps in-game coordinates to that center.

Use `/earthmap climate` in-game at your player position to inspect the twelve
monthly mean temperatures (January–December), annual mean temperature,
annual P and PET (mm/year), effective rainfall (the vegetation wetness value),
its P/(P+PET) and P/200 cap inputs, and the separate weather rain factor
P/(P+800). The command requires the server-control privilege. EMCL stores
monthly temperatures but only **annual** P and PET, so monthly rainfall and PET
cannot be reported. `/earthmap pos` remains available for terrain diagnostics.

Earth terrain is filled before Vintage Story's rock-strata pass, so the game's
procedural rock layers can replace the base granite. Ore generation runs in
the later terrain-features pass. Changing this mod's worldgen order affects
only newly generated chunks; regenerate existing chunks to replace their
old all-granite terrain and rerun deposit generation.

Limitations
-------------------
- Map generation must be a multiple of 512 blocks.
- The region height, river-surface and ocean-bed planes remain 8-bit source Y;
  taller worlds stretch that resolution rather than gaining additional detail.
- Rock types and ore locations use game/mod procedural generation; the Earth
  data does not contain real-world lithology or mineral-deposit locations.
- Vintage Story map resolutions/padding are hardcoded on the client.

